coder › Done on branch `work/json-flag` (3 commits on top of `bc6079e`).

What changed:
- New `src/csv.ts` exporting `parseCsv(text: string): string[][]`. Splits on `\n`, strips one trailing `\r` per line (CRLF), drops a single trailing newline. Per line: quote opens a quoted section only at field start; inside quotes `""` → `"`, commas are literal; otherwise fields split on `,`. Matches the existing small-module style in `src/cli.ts`.
- `src/cli.ts`: added `--json` handling — first row is the header, each data row becomes an object keyed by header names (values stay strings), printed as `JSON.stringify(objs) + "\n"`. Empty input or header-only prints `[]\n`. A row whose field count differs from the header returns `{ out: "error: line N has X fields, expected Y\n", code: 1 }` with 1-based N, no JSON. Usage line now `usage: csvtool --upper | --count | --json`.
- Tests: new `src/csv.test.ts` (empty, simple, CRLF, quoted comma, doubled quote); extended `src/cli.test.ts` (usage text, basic conversion, string values, quotes+commas+CRLF, empty and header-only, ragged rows on lines 2 and 3 naming the line).

Decisions not in the plan: `--json` is checked after `--upper`/`--count` (same precedence pattern as the existing flags); the error line format `error: line N has X fields, expected Y` — the spec only required naming the line, so I picked this wording; unquoted quotes mid-field are treated as literal characters.

Checks:
- `bun test`: 15 pass, 0 fail across 2 files (was 3 pass before).
- `tsc --noEmit` (ad-hoc flags, since repo has no tsconfig/node_modules): exit 0, no errors. No lint configured (`bun test` is the only script).

